\begin_layout Standard
Unlike modern electronic datasets that output long CSV files the 1972 Freedman-Clauser experiment
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Freedman1972"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 was conducted using hardwired,
 discrete nuclear-physics counting modules (NIM bins).
 The data was published as a fixed set of compiled coincidence ratios across nine distinct relative angles 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\varphi\right)$
\end_inset

 rather than individual time-tagged lines.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The primary paper,
 published in Physical Review Letters (Vol.
 28,
 No.
 14),
 condenses these 200 hours of laboratory runs into the explicit normalized coincidence rate table.
 The table represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{R(φ)}{R₀}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $R₀$
\end_inset

 is the master reference rate measured with both polarizing filters completely removed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The 1972 Freedman-Clauser Normalized Data Table
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The experiment measured the 
\begin_inset Formula $J=0\rightarrow J=1\rightarrow J=0$
\end_inset

 atomic cascade of calcium atoms.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Due to rotational symmetry the coincidence rate depends strictly on the relative angle 
\begin_inset Formula $φ=|a-b|$
\end_inset

 between the two filters:
\end_layout

