\begin_layout Section
The Core Hardware Calibration Constants
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To simulate or parse this run inside an Ada program using  Kolmogorov discrete probability metrics,
 you need the exact experimental efficiency boundaries recorded by their "pile-of-plates" polarizers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
R₀ Baseline Reference Rate:
 Approximately 0.28 to 0.30 coincidences per second with no polarizers inserted.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Maximum Filter Transmittance 
\begin_inset Formula $(ε₁¹,ε₂¹)$
\end_inset

:
 0.97 ± 0.01
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Minimum Filter Transmittance 
\begin_inset Formula $(ε₁²,ε₂²)$
\end_inset

:
 0.038 ± 0.004
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Solid Angle Factor (
\begin_inset Formula $F(0)):0.984$
\end_inset

) (Accounts for the physical size of the collecting lenses).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
How This Applies directly to the Freedom Parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $(δ)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the paper,
 Freedman contracted the CHSH inequality down into his own specific physical variation 
\begin_inset Formula $(δ≤0)$
\end_inset

 using the symmetric values from the critical 
\begin_inset Formula $22.5°$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $67.5°$
\end_inset

orientations:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
δ & = & \frac{|R(22.5∘)−R(67.5∘)|}{R_{0}}−\frac{1}{4}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Plugging their raw table ratios into this formula yields:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\delta_{\text{exp}} & = & \left|0.364-0.093\right|-0.25=0.271-0.25\\
 & = & \mathbf{0.021}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because 0.021 sits 6.5 standard deviations above the strict local realistic limit of zero they claimed a violation of Bell's theorem.
 
\end_layout

